>> title: Scrapple
>> category: Fried & Crispy
>> tags: pork, cornmeal, buckwheat, breakfast, make-ahead
>> counters: Diner
>> aka: pon haus, pannhaas, panhaas, panhoss, Philadelphia scrapple, pan haus
>> servings: 12
>> time: 11 hr
>> slack: narrow — the mush is cooked until a spoon stands in it; poured out loose it sets soft and falls apart in the pan the next morning
>> step.1: simmer 2 hr, until the meat gives up the bone
>> step.2: strain the broth, shred the meat
>> step.3: whisk in, cook 25 min, until a spoon stands
>> step.4: season, pour into the pan, chill 8 hr
>> step.5: slice thin, fry hard 10 min

Simmer @pork shoulder{2%lb}(900 g; in large pieces), @pork neck bones{1%lb}(450 g), @yellow onion{1%large}(halved), @bay leaves{2} and @black peppercorns{1%tsp}(2 g) in @water{3%qt}(2.8 L) ~simmer{2%hr}, skimming the first grey foam off, until the meat falls off the bone.

Strain @&(~1)pork and broth{}, keeping 6 cups (1.4 L) of the broth, and shred the meat fine with two forks, discarding the bones and the aromatics.

Whisk @stone-ground yellow cornmeal{2%cup}(280 g) and @buckwheat flour{1/2%cup}(60 g) into @&(~1)shredded pork and broth{} in a slow rain, then cook it over low heat ~simmer{25%min}, stirring often, until a spoon stands up in it. It spits like lava — use a long spoon and a deep pot.

Season @&(~1)scrapple mush{} with @rubbed sage{1%Tbs}(3 g), @dried thyme{2%tsp}(2 g), @dried savory{1%tsp}(1 g), @kosher salt{1%Tbs}(18 g) and @coarse black pepper{2%tsp}(4 g), pour it into a #loaf pan{} lined with plastic, and ~chill{8%hr} until it sets firm enough to slice.

Slice @&(~1)set scrapple{} 3/8-in thick and fry in @bacon fat{2%Tbs}(25 g) in a #cast-iron skillet{} over medium heat ~fry{10%min}, turning once, until both faces are dark and crisp. No flour on the slice: flour makes a crust on a loaf that is still wet inside, which is the usual way scrapple gets a bad name.
